standards.push({
  slug: 'api-1104',
  code: 'API 1104',
  displayTitle: 'API 1104 — Welding of Pipelines and Related Facilities',
  shortDescription: 'Welding code for cross-country pipeline construction — qualifies WPS/welders for oil and gas pipelines, sets RT/UT acceptance for girth welds.',
  scope: `<p>API 1104 — <strong>Welding of Pipelines and Related Facilities</strong> — is the welding code for cross-country pipeline construction. It is the dominant code for oil and gas transmission pipelines in North America and is widely referenced internationally for pipelines transporting crude petroleum, petroleum products, fuel gases, carbon dioxide, nitrogen, and other gases. API 1104 covers welding procedure qualification (WPS/PQR), welder qualification, production welding requirements, examination (visual, radiographic, ultrasonic), and acceptance criteria for girth welds and other field welds.</p><p>API 1104 differs from ASME Section IX in welder qualification details and from ASME B31.4/B31.8 in acceptance criteria — it is specifically tuned to the high-volume, field-erection environment of cross-country pipeline construction. The current edition is the 22nd Edition (October 2021) with Errata.</p>`,
  keyRequirements: `<h2>Scope &amp; Application (1)</h2><p>API 1104 applies to: arc welding and oxyacetylene welding for new construction and repair of pipelines, related facilities (pump stations, compressor stations, valve stations, metering and pressure-regulating stations), and tie-in/replacement welds in existing pipeline systems. Outside the scope: pressure vessels (use ASME VIII), tankage (use API 650), and process piping (use B31.3).</p><h2>Welding Procedure Specification — WPS (5)</h2><p>The WPS shall be qualified by Procedure Qualification Record (PQR). PQR is the record of test welds and the destructive tests (tensile, bend, nick-break, Charpy) performed to qualify the procedure. Essential variables include: process (SMAW, GMAW, GTAW, FCAW), filler metal classification, pipe diameter and wall thickness, material grade (X42, X52, X65, X70, X80), preheat, interpass temperature, postweld heat treatment if any, joint configuration. A change in any essential variable requires PQR requalification.</p><h2>Welder Qualification (6)</h2><p>Each welder shall be qualified by performance test — typically welding a test joint identical to the production joint. Acceptance is by destructive testing of the test weld: tensile strength, nick-break, side-bend, and root-bend test results per Section 6.5. Welders are qualified for specific position (1G, 2G, 5G, 6G), specific pipe diameter range, and specific wall-thickness range. Cross-country pipeline welders typically qualify in 5G or 6G position for fixed-position welding from outside the pipe.</p><h2>Inspector Qualification (Annex A)</h2><p>Examiners performing visual examination and radiographic interpretation shall be qualified per API 1104 Annex A — typically AWS-CWI (welding inspector) certification, ASNT Level II RT, or equivalent. RT film interpretation requires Level II RT certification.</p><h2>Visual Inspection (9.3)</h2><p>Visual examination is performed by the inspector at the joint after welding and prior to other NDE. Acceptance includes weld profile (height of bead, width), undercut, surface porosity, and surface cracks. Per API 1104 9.3, defects rejectable by visual inspection: any external cracks, undercut exceeding limits, lack of fusion at the cap, incomplete penetration, burn-through, excessive reinforcement, and concavity below specified.</p><h2>Radiographic Inspection (9.5)</h2><p>RT is the primary volumetric examination for cross-country pipeline girth welds. Per API 1104 9.5, examination shall be performed with Ir-192 or X-ray, with film density 2.0 to 4.0 (gamma) or 1.8 to 4.0 (X-ray), wire IQI per Table 9.5.1.1 (typical 35 wire IQI for 0.5 in WT). 100% coverage of the girth weld is standard for new construction; 10% sampling is permitted for some service classes per operator policy.</p><h2>RT Acceptance Criteria (9.3.6)</h2><p>API 1104 acceptance is more permissive than ASME for similar weld thickness because pipeline service is uniaxial tension (hoop stress). Key limits:</p><ul><li><strong>Cracks:</strong> Not acceptable.</li><li><strong>Lack of fusion (LOF):</strong> Maximum 1 in any continuous 12-in weld; aggregate maximum 2 in per 12-in weld.</li><li><strong>Incomplete penetration (IP):</strong> Maximum 1 in any continuous 12-in weld with depth ≤ 0.030 in.</li><li><strong>Burn-through:</strong> Maximum depth and length per Table.</li><li><strong>Slag:</strong> Aggregate length max 2 in per 12-in weld; isolated max 1/2 in.</li><li><strong>Porosity:</strong> Cluster porosity allowable per Chart 9.3.8; aggregate density limits.</li></ul><h2>Ultrasonic Testing — Alternative to RT (11)</h2><p>API 1104 Section 11 permits AUT (automated ultrasonic testing) in lieu of RT for girth welds. AUT systems use zonal coverage with multiple PAUT focal laws covering specific weld bevels (root, hot pass, fill, cap). AUT is the dominant inspection method for offshore pipelines and increasingly for onshore in remote locations. Calibration is performed on a representative pipe sample with notches and side-drilled reflectors. Acceptance is per Section 11 — typically based on amplitude and length relative to calibrated reflectors.</p><h2>Mechanized Welding (Annex E)</h2><p>Annex E covers mechanized welding processes (e.g., CRC-Evans, SAW). Procedure qualification follows the same framework but with provisions for automated parameter control.</p><h2>Repair (10)</h2><p>Repairs are categorized as: minor (small porosity, slag), small (less than 30% wall thickness), and large (greater than 30% wall thickness). Repair procedures require qualification; multiple repairs at the same location are limited (typically 2 repairs maximum before cut-out). All repairs are re-inspected after completion.</p><h2>Common Audit Findings</h2><p>(a) Welder qualification expired (typically 6 months without qualified production weld). (b) WPS essential variables changed without requalification. (c) Film density outside 2.0-4.0 (gamma) limits. (d) IQI wire essential not visible — re-shoot required. (e) AUT calibration sample not matched to production material. (f) Repair limit exceeded (3rd repair attempted). (g) Visual examiner not qualified per Annex A.</p>`,
  revisionsHistory: [
    { year: '2021', notes: '22nd Edition; expanded Section 11 AUT requirements; updated Annex E mechanized welding.' },
    { year: '2018', notes: '21st Edition; revised acceptance limits for porosity and slag.' },
    { year: '2013', notes: '20th Edition; added requirements for high-strength steel grades X70 and above.' },
    { year: '2010', notes: '20th Edition (interim).' },
  ],
  relatedStandards: ['api-570', 'aws-d1-1', 'asme-b31-3', 'asme-section-v-article-2', 'asme-section-v-article-4', 'iso-17636', 'iso-17640'],
  applicableCertifications: [
    { label: 'ASNT Certification (RT, UT)', href: CERTS.asnt },
    { label: 'API 570 Certification', href: CERTS.api570 },
  ],
  applicableMethods: [
    { label: 'Pipeline Integrity Inspection', href: METHODS.pipe },
    { label: 'Weld Inspection Methods', href: METHODS.weld },
    { label: 'Radiographic Testing (RT)', href: METHODS.rt },
    { label: 'Phased Array UT', href: METHODS.paut },
  ],
  issuingBody: 'API',
  category: 'code',
});

