standards.push({
  slug: 'astm-e94',
  code: 'ASTM E94',
  displayTitle: 'ASTM E94 — Standard Guide for Radiographic Examination',
  shortDescription: 'General guide for radiographic examination — covers technique selection, equipment, materials, and procedure development for industrial RT.',
  scope: `<p>ASTM E94 — <strong>Standard Guide for Radiographic Examination Using Industrial Radiographic Film</strong> — is the foundational ASTM general guide for radiographic examination. While not a directly invoking acceptance standard, E94 provides the technical framework for industrial radiography that other ASTM and ASME standards build upon. It covers source selection, source-to-film distance, IQI selection, film selection (speed and contrast classes), processing, and viewing — applicable across welded steel, castings, and base materials.</p><p>ASTM E94 is referenced by ASME Section V Article 2 as SE-94, by ASTM E165 (weld RT for non-ferrous), by E1742 (radiologic interpretation), and many specialized RT standards. The current published version is ASTM E94/E94M-17 (reapproved 2023). Coverage spans both X-ray (kilovoltage) and gamma-ray (Ir-192, Co-60, Se-75) sources.</p>`,
  keyRequirements: `<h2>Film Classes (4.2)</h2><p>ASTM E94 categorizes industrial radiographic film into four classes: Class I (special, slowest, finest grain, highest contrast — e.g., Kodak Industrex T200, Agfa D2), Class II (fine grain, medium speed — D4), Class III (high speed, coarser grain — D7), and Class WT (water-resistant). Class selection depends on contrast and resolution requirements vs. exposure time tolerance. Most industrial weld inspection uses Class II film.</p><h2>Source Selection by Thickness (6)</h2><p>Section 6 guidance for source selection:</p><ul><li>X-ray below 150 kV: very thin sections, aluminum, and light alloy.</li><li>X-ray 150-300 kV: typical steel up to 1.5 in.</li><li>X-ray 300-450 kV: steel 1 to 4 in; megavoltage above for thicker.</li><li>Se-75 gamma: 0.25-1.5 in steel.</li><li>Ir-192 gamma: 0.5-3 in steel.</li><li>Co-60 gamma: 1.5-9 in steel.</li></ul><h2>Source-to-Film Distance (7.2)</h2><p>SFD selection considers geometric unsharpness and density variation. SFD shall be at least 7-10× the source-side thickness for typical applications. For 1 in steel, SFD ≥ 24 in is common. Larger SFD reduces unsharpness and density variation but extends exposure time proportional to (SFD)².</p><h2>IQI Selection (8)</h2><p>ASTM E94 references ASTM E747 (wire IQI) and ASTM E1025 (hole-type IQI). IQI material group shall match the part material group. Essential IQI hole or wire shall be visible on the radiograph. For weld examination, IQI is placed on the weld or adjacent to it; for plate examination, IQI is centered in the area of interest.</p><h2>Geometric Unsharpness (7.1)</h2><p>Ug = F × d / D, with limits 0.020 in for steel below 2 in, 0.030 in for 2-3 in, 0.040 in for 3-4 in, and 0.070 in above 4 in. Source focal-spot size F is documented for X-ray (typically 2-3 mm) and inherent in gamma source design (Ir-192 typical 2-3 mm).</p><h2>Film Density (10.2)</h2><p>Per ASTM E94, single-film density 1.8 to 4.0 for X-ray, 2.0 to 4.0 for gamma. Composite (two films) 1.3 each, 2.6 composite minimum.</p><h2>Lead Screens (9)</h2><p>Lead intensifying screens are standard for industrial RT. Typical thicknesses: 0.005 in (0.127 mm) front, 0.010 in (0.254 mm) back. Lead screens enhance image quality by photoelectric absorption of scattered radiation and provide additional photoelectrons. Fluorescent screens are not generally recommended for industrial RT due to image quality compromise.</p><h2>Backscatter Verification (9.4)</h2><p>A lead character "B" of specified dimensions is placed on the back of the cassette. If the "B" appears lighter than background, backscatter exposure is excessive; additional lead backing is required behind the cassette.</p><h2>Processing (10)</h2><p>ASTM E94 references ASTM E999 for processing — automated processors are standard. Developer temperature, time, and chemistry are controlled. Manual processing remains acceptable for field RT and small-volume facilities, with similar process control.</p><h2>Identification of Radiographs (11)</h2><p>Each radiograph shall be uniquely identified with lead characters exposed onto the film: part number, weld number or position, date, station, technique number, and IQI identification. Pen-written identification after development is not acceptable for permanent records.</p><h2>Viewing (12)</h2><p>Viewers (illuminators) shall provide adequate illumination of the highest-density area of the film. Variable-intensity illuminators (e.g., Trojan or equivalent) are used. Ambient light shall be controlled — the viewing room should be darkened to maintain operator dark-adaptation.</p><h2>Application — Reference vs. Standard</h2><p>ASTM E94 is a "Guide" — non-mandatory unless invoked by a specifying standard. In ASME Section V Article 2 (where ASTM E94 is adopted as SE-94), the provisions become mandatory for those aspects specifically referenced. In non-ASME applications (e.g., ASTM E165 for welds), E94 guides technique selection but acceptance is per E165 or the customer's specification.</p><h2>Digital Radiography (Reference to E2698, E2737)</h2><p>For computed radiography (CR), refer to ASTM E2445 and E2446. For digital radiography (DR), refer to ASTM E2698 and E2737. ASTM E94 itself addresses film-based RT; the supplementary DR/CR standards extend the framework to digital media.</p><h2>Common Audit Findings</h2><p>(a) Density at edge of film below 1.8 (X-ray) or 2.0 (gamma). (b) Lead identification missing or pen-written after development. (c) IQI material group mismatched to part. (d) Backscatter "B" indicates inadequate backing. (e) SFD too short — unsharpness exceeds limit. (f) Source focal-spot size not documented for unsharpness calculation. (g) Film processing parameters not controlled.</p>`,
  revisionsHistory: [
    { year: '2023', notes: 'Reapproval of E94-17; minor editorial updates; added cross-references to digital RT standards.' },
    { year: '2017', notes: 'E94-17 published; updated film class descriptions to align with current manufacturer product lines.' },
    { year: '2010', notes: 'Previous major revision; aligned terminology with current ASME Section V.' },
  ],
  relatedStandards: ['astm-e165', 'astm-e1417', 'astm-e1444', 'asme-section-v-article-2', 'iso-17636'],
  applicableCertifications: [
    { label: 'ASNT Certification (RT Levels I, II, III)', href: CERTS.asnt },
  ],
  applicableMethods: [
    { label: 'Radiographic Testing (RT)', href: METHODS.rt },
    { label: 'ASTM E165 Radiographic Standards', href: METHODS.astme165 },
  ],
  issuingBody: 'ASTM',
  category: 'guide',
});

standards.push({
  slug: 'astm-e165',
  code: 'ASTM E165',
  displayTitle: 'ASTM E165 — Standard Practice for Liquid Penetrant Examination',
  shortDescription: 'General industrial liquid penetrant testing practice — covers visible and fluorescent PT methods, surface preparation, dwell time, and process control.',
  scope: `<p>ASTM E165 — <strong>Standard Practice for Liquid Penetrant Examination for General Industry</strong> — is the foundational ASTM standard for industrial PT. It is broader than ASTM E1417 (which is aerospace-specific with stricter sensitivity classification) but covers the same fundamental physics and process control. E165 is adopted by ASME Section V Article 6 as SE-165. E165 covers visible (Type II) and fluorescent (Type I) penetrants, and Type C solvent-removable, Type B water-washable, and Type A/D post-emulsifiable methods.</p><p>The current edition is ASTM E165/E165M-23 (most recent). E165 references E1316 (terminology), E1417 (aerospace PT), and the Annex covering penetrant qualification testing.</p>`,
  keyRequirements: `<h2>Penetrant Classification (5)</h2><ul><li><strong>Type I — Fluorescent:</strong> Penetrants that fluoresce under UV-A. Higher sensitivity than visible. Used in aerospace and high-criticality applications.</li><li><strong>Type II — Visible:</strong> Color-contrast (typically red) penetrants visible under white light. Lower sensitivity but more practical for field work.</li><li><strong>Method A — Water-washable:</strong> Penetrant contains emulsifier; rinsed with water spray.</li><li><strong>Method B — Post-emulsifiable, lipophilic:</strong> Oil-based emulsifier applied separately. Higher sensitivity than Method A.</li><li><strong>Method C — Solvent-removable:</strong> Excess penetrant wiped with solvent-dampened cloth. Field practical.</li><li><strong>Method D — Post-emulsifiable, hydrophilic:</strong> Water-soluble emulsifier. Most controllable.</li></ul><h2>Procedure Essential Variables (6.1)</h2><p>Procedure shall address: penetrant family (penetrant, emulsifier, remover, developer of the same family), surface preparation method, penetrant application method, dwell time, removal of excess, developer application, dwell time of developer, inspection method, lighting requirements, surface temperature range.</p><h2>Surface Preparation (8)</h2><p>The surface and surrounding area (1 in beyond area of interest) shall be clean and free of: scale, slag, rust, paint, oil, grease, coatings, and contaminants that mask discontinuities. Cleaning methods: detergent, solvent (alcohol, acetone), vapor degreasing, ultrasonic, mechanical (consistent with not closing discontinuities). Mechanical preparation that closes flaws — peening, abrasive blasting at high velocity — is prohibited as final preparation.</p><h2>Material Compatibility (8.4)</h2><p>For austenitic stainless steel, nickel alloys, and titanium, penetrant chlorine, fluorine, and sulfur content is limited (typically each less than 1% by weight or cumulative 1%). Penetrant material certification accompanies the procedure. Standard tests: ASTM E165 Annex A2 (halogen content) and Annex A3 (sulfur content).</p><h2>Penetration (Dwell) Time (9)</h2><p>Per ASTM E165 Table 1, minimum dwell time depends on material and discontinuity type:</p><ul><li><strong>Aluminum, magnesium:</strong> 5 min castings/extrusions, 10 min welds.</li><li><strong>Steel:</strong> 5 min castings/extrusions, 10 min welds, fatigue cracks.</li><li><strong>Titanium / high-temperature alloys:</strong> 20-30 min depending on Type/Method.</li><li><strong>Plastics:</strong> 5 min.</li></ul><p>Maximum dwell typically 1 hour; longer dwell risks penetrant drying on the surface.</p><h2>Excess Penetrant Removal (10)</h2><p>Water-washable: water spray at 40 psi maximum, 110°F maximum. Solvent removal: wipe dry first, then wipe with solvent-dampened cloth. Direct solvent application to part is prohibited. Post-emulsifiable: emulsifier dwell per manufacturer (30 sec to 2 min), then water rinse.</p><h2>Developer (11)</h2><p>Dry powder, aqueous wet, or non-aqueous wet (solvent-based). Non-aqueous wet developer is the most sensitive. Developer dwell time minimum 7 min and not less than penetrant dwell time. Excess developer is left on the part during inspection.</p><h2>Lighting Requirements (12)</h2><p>Type II visible PT: ≥ 100 fc (1076 lux) white light at the part surface, glare-free, measured with calibrated light meter.</p><p>Type I fluorescent PT: ≤ 2 fc ambient white light, ≥ 1000 µW/cm² UV-A at the part surface, peak UV-A 365 nm. Examiner dark-adaptation 1 minute before inspection.</p><h2>Inspector Vision (4)</h2><p>Inspectors shall pass an annual near-distance acuity test (Jaeger J1 or equivalent at 12 in) and a color contrast / color vision test (Ishihara or equivalent). For fluorescent PT, the examiner shall verify the ability to detect fluorescence at the lower limit of expected fluorescence (typically using a UV-fluorescing reference standard).</p><h2>Process Control (Annex A1)</h2><p>Daily process control checks: UV-A intensity verification, white light intensity verification, visual examination of penetrant materials for contamination (water content, sludge), centrifuge tube fluid concentration check (post-emulsifiable systems), and reference panel verification (TAM panel or PSM-5 panel). Process control records are retained.</p><h2>Acceptance Criteria</h2><p>ASTM E165 itself does not define accept/reject criteria; the referencing standard or contract specification defines. Typical limits in referencing standards: linear indications with length &gt; 3× width are characterized as cracks/lack of fusion and are not acceptable. Rounded indications larger than specified size are not acceptable.</p><h2>Common Audit Findings</h2><p>(a) Halogen/sulfur content not certified for stainless steel application. (b) UV-A intensity below 1000 µW/cm² at part surface. (c) Examiner dark-adaptation pause skipped. (d) Penetrant families mixed across the four chemistries. (e) Process control checks not performed. (f) Reference panel verification missing. (g) Dwell time below table minimum.</p>`,
  revisionsHistory: [
    { year: '2023', notes: 'E165/E165M-23 published; updated UV-A specifications; aligned with ISO 3452-1 latest revision.' },
    { year: '2018', notes: 'E165/E165M-18; clarified Type/Method classification.' },
    { year: '2012', notes: 'Previous major revision; expanded Annex A1 process control requirements.' },
  ],
  relatedStandards: ['astm-e1417', 'astm-e94', 'asme-section-v-article-6', 'iso-3452'],
  applicableCertifications: [
    { label: 'ASNT Certification (PT Levels I, II, III)', href: CERTS.asnt },
  ],
  applicableMethods: [
    { label: 'Liquid Penetrant Testing (PT)', href: METHODS.pt },
    { label: 'ISO 3452 PT Specification', href: METHODS.iso3452 },
    { label: 'ASTM E165 RT Standards Reference (related)', href: METHODS.astme165 },
  ],
  issuingBody: 'ASTM',
  category: 'practice',
});

standards.push({
  slug: 'astm-e709',
  code: 'ASTM E709',
  displayTitle: 'ASTM E709 — Standard Guide for Magnetic Particle Testing',
  shortDescription: 'General guide for magnetic particle examination — covers all magnetization techniques, particle systems, lighting, and process control.',
  scope: `<p>ASTM E709 — <strong>Standard Guide for Magnetic Particle Testing</strong> — is the foundational ASTM guide for MT/MPI for industrial applications. It is adopted by ASME Section V Article 7 as SE-709. ASTM E709 covers all MT techniques: yoke (AC, DC, half-wave DC), prod, coil, central conductor, head shot, and magnetic rubber inspection. It addresses both dry-powder and wet-suspension particle systems, in visible and fluorescent forms.</p><p>The current edition is ASTM E709-21 (most recent). E709 is the methodology reference; ASTM E1444 is the aerospace-specific MT standard with stricter classifications. Both reference E1316 (NDE terminology).</p>`,
  keyRequirements: `<h2>Magnetization Techniques (8)</h2><p><strong>Yoke method:</strong> AC or DC electromagnetic yoke. AC yoke shall lift 10 lb test piece at maximum pole spacing per ASTM E709 8.6. DC yoke shall lift 40 lb. AC yoke is preferred for surface defects; DC for subsurface.</p><p><strong>Prod method:</strong> Two electrodes contact the part. Current 100-125 A/in of prod spacing. Spacing 3-8 in typical. Prod method is prohibited on critical aerospace and many high-quality applications due to arc burn risk.</p><p><strong>Coil method:</strong> Encircling coil produces longitudinal field. Effective field length 6-9 in for single coil. Coil ampere-turns calculated per ASTM E709 8.4. Multiple shots required for long parts.</p><p><strong>Central conductor:</strong> Bar passed through hollow part. Field strength at OD surface is the controlling parameter. Current = circumference × specific Amp/in.</p><p><strong>Head shot (direct contact):</strong> Current passed through part via contact heads. Generates circumferential field around the part. Less common due to arc burn risk.</p><h2>Two-Direction Examination (5.6)</h2><p>Indications oriented parallel to the magnetic field are not detected. Two examinations at approximately 90° (longitudinal + circumferential fields) are required to detect indications of any orientation. The procedure shall specify both field directions and the order.</p><h2>Particle Selection (10)</h2><p><strong>Dry particles:</strong> Iron oxide powders, applied by powder bulb or shaker. Color variants (red, black, gray) provide contrast with the part. Used with the yoke and prod methods.</p><p><strong>Wet particles:</strong> Particle suspension in oil or water with surfactants. Concentration controlled by centrifuge tube (0.1-0.4 mL settled per 100 mL for visible, 0.1-0.4 mL or less for fluorescent). Wet method is more sensitive than dry for fine cracks.</p><p><strong>Fluorescent vs. visible:</strong> Fluorescent particles fluoresce green-yellow under UV-A (365 nm peak). Higher sensitivity than visible; requires darkened booth.</p><h2>Field Strength Verification (8.11)</h2><p>Field strength is verified by: (1) Hall-effect tangential field gauge, ≥ 30 Gauss (some applications require 60 Gauss minimum). (2) Pie-shaped field indicator (Berthold gauge) — shows the field direction and approximate strength. (3) Magnetic flux indicator (MFI) strips — shows field on the part itself. (4) Reference standard (Ketos ring, MTU-1 or equivalent) — controlled artifact for system verification.</p><h2>Lighting Requirements (11)</h2><p>Visible MT: ≥ 100 fc (1076 lux) white light at the part surface.</p><p>Fluorescent MT: ≤ 2 fc ambient white light, ≥ 1000 µW/cm² UV-A at the part surface, peak emission 365 nm. Examiner dark-adaptation 1 minute minimum.</p><h2>Surface Preparation (7)</h2><p>The surface shall be clean and free of heavy scale, slag, paint, rust, and contaminants that interfere with particle mobility. Mill scale is removed; light surface roughness is acceptable for dry-powder MT. Wet method requires smoother surface.</p><h2>Demagnetization (13)</h2><p>Demagnetization is required when residual magnetism interferes with subsequent processes (welding, machining, instrumentation, or bearing service). AC demagnetization is the most effective; DC reversing demagnetization is used for thick sections. Residual field shall be reduced to acceptable levels — typically ≤ 3 Gauss.</p><h2>Continuous vs. Residual Method (5.4)</h2><p>Continuous method: magnetization and particle application overlap — particles are applied while the field is active. Higher sensitivity for fine flaws.</p><p>Residual method: particles applied after magnetization. Used on high-permeability materials where residual field is sufficient. Lower sensitivity but useful for hollow parts where in-place magnetization is difficult.</p><h2>Process Control (Annex)</h2><p>Daily process control: light intensity, UV-A intensity, particle concentration (centrifuge tube), reference standard verification, system verification on Ketos ring or equivalent. Records retained.</p><h2>Acceptance Criteria</h2><p>ASTM E709 itself does not define accept/reject criteria. The referencing standard or contract specification defines. Typical criteria: linear indications greater than 1/16 in are evaluated against length and width; rounded indications larger than specified are evaluated; cracks are rejectable regardless of length.</p><h2>Common Audit Findings</h2><p>(a) Two-direction examination not performed. (b) Field strength verification missing or expired calibration. (c) Particle concentration not within range (centrifuge tube check). (d) Hall gauge calibration expired. (e) Prod arc burns not addressed. (f) Demagnetization missing before subsequent welding. (g) UV-A intensity below 1000 µW/cm².</p>`,
  revisionsHistory: [
    { year: '2021', notes: 'E709-21 published; updated UV-A peak emission specification; aligned with ASTM E1444 latest revision.' },
    { year: '2015', notes: 'E709-15; expanded process control requirements.' },
    { year: '2008', notes: 'Major revision; restructured for clarity.' },
  ],
  relatedStandards: ['astm-e1444', 'asme-section-v-article-7', 'iso-9934'],
  applicableCertifications: [
    { label: 'ASNT Certification (MT Levels I, II, III)', href: CERTS.asnt },
  ],
  applicableMethods: [
    { label: 'Magnetic Particle Testing (MT)', href: METHODS.mt },
    { label: 'ISO 9934 MT Standards', href: METHODS.iso9934 },
  ],
  issuingBody: 'ASTM',
  category: 'guide',
});

standards.push({
  slug: 'astm-e1417',
  code: 'ASTM E1417',
  displayTitle: 'ASTM E1417 — Standard Practice for Liquid Penetrant Testing',
  shortDescription: 'Aerospace-grade liquid penetrant practice — defines sensitivity Levels 1/2/3/4, qualified products list (QPL), and rigorous process control for safety-critical components.',
  scope: `<p>ASTM E1417 — <strong>Standard Practice for Liquid Penetrant Testing</strong> — is the aerospace-grade PT practice with stricter requirements than ASTM E165. E1417 is the dominant PT standard for aerospace components (Boeing, Airbus, Pratt &amp; Whitney, GE Aerospace, Rolls-Royce flow-down), nuclear components, and other safety-critical applications. It defines sensitivity levels (1, 2, 3, 4), references the qualified-products list (QPL) for penetrant materials, and imposes rigorous daily process control.</p><p>The current edition is ASTM E1417/E1417M-21. E1417 is more demanding than E165 in: sensitivity classification, qualified materials, process control frequency, system verification methods (TAM panel + PSM-5 panel), and audit traceability. Components subject to E1417 are typically rotating engine parts, primary structure, and high-cycle-fatigue components.</p>`,
  keyRequirements: `<h2>Sensitivity Level Classification (5)</h2><p>Penetrants are qualified to one of four sensitivity levels:</p><ul><li><strong>Level 1 — Low sensitivity:</strong> Visible (Type II) penetrants, used for surface flaws not requiring high resolution.</li><li><strong>Level 2 — Medium sensitivity:</strong> Fluorescent (Type I) general purpose.</li><li><strong>Level 3 — High sensitivity:</strong> Fluorescent for fine-flaw detection (e.g., fatigue cracks in turbine blades).</li><li><strong>Level 4 — Ultra-high sensitivity:</strong> Highest sensitivity fluorescent, for the finest tight cracks.</li></ul><p>Sensitivity is verified using a reference panel — TAM panel (Type I/II) or PSM-5 panel — that contains seeded cracks of known dimensions.</p><h2>Qualified Products List (4.3)</h2><p>Penetrant materials shall be from a qualified products list (QPL) — typically AMS 2644 (Aerospace Material Specification) listed materials. Each penetrant family (penetrant + emulsifier + remover + developer) is qualified together; mixing across families is prohibited.</p><h2>Penetrant Family Compatibility (6.1)</h2><p>All four components shall be of the same family and brand. Substitution requires re-qualification of the procedure. Process variation tracking via barcode or batch lot ID is typical in aerospace facilities.</p><h2>Daily Process Control (Annex A1)</h2><p>Per ASTM E1417 Annex A1, daily process control checks include:</p><ul><li><strong>UV-A intensity:</strong> Minimum 1000 µW/cm² at the part surface for fluorescent. Verified at start of shift with calibrated radiometer.</li><li><strong>White light intensity:</strong> Maximum 2 fc ambient in fluorescent inspection area.</li><li><strong>Reference panel verification:</strong> TAM panel or PSM-5 with known cracks — system shall detect specified crack pattern.</li><li><strong>Penetrant performance:</strong> Pass/fail verification against the panel.</li><li><strong>Bath concentration (post-emulsifiable):</strong> Centrifuge tube settled volume within range.</li><li><strong>Water content (water-washable):</strong> Maximum 5% water in oil-based penetrant — verified by centrifuge or refractometer.</li><li><strong>Emulsifier concentration:</strong> Refractometer reading or titration per manufacturer.</li></ul><p>Process control records are retained typically 7 years (aerospace) or per the operator's QMS.</p><h2>System Verification (Annex A2)</h2><p>Monthly or per-procedure interval verification: complete process performed on a known cracked panel. The system shall reveal the cracks specified by the panel manufacturer. If the system fails, the cause is investigated and re-verified.</p><h2>Surface Preparation (8)</h2><p>For aerospace components, surface preparation is rigorous. Etching is commonly required before PT on machined surfaces — alkaline or acidic etch removes smeared metal that has closed flaws. Etching is followed by neutralization, rinse, and drying before penetrant application.</p><h2>Dwell Time (9)</h2><p>ASTM E1417 dwell time minimums are similar to E165 but typically extended for high-criticality aerospace components: 10 min for typical Level 2 application, 15-30 min for Level 3, up to 60 min for Level 4 sensitivity on titanium and nickel-alloy components.</p><h2>Excess Penetrant Removal (10)</h2><p>Water-washable: water spray at 40 psi maximum, 110°F maximum. Pressure gauge and temperature gauge in the wash station. Over-rinse risk: removes penetrant from flaws — operator training and rinse time control mitigates.</p><p>Post-emulsifiable: emulsifier dwell typically 30 sec to 2 min, controlled with stopwatch. Over-emulsification penetrates into flaws and removes penetrant — strict time control.</p><h2>Developer (11)</h2><p>Non-aqueous wet developer is the highest-sensitivity option. Aqueous wet developer (solvent-free) is permitted. Dry developer is used for visible Level 1.</p><h2>Examiner Qualification (4.2)</h2><p>Examiners shall be certified per the customer's certification system — typically NAS 410 for aerospace, ASNT SNT-TC-1A or CP-189 for non-aerospace. Annual vision exams (near-distance acuity and color contrast). For Level 4 ultra-high sensitivity, additional examiner training/demonstration may be required.</p><h2>Material Compatibility</h2><p>For titanium, nickel alloys, and other high-temperature aerospace alloys, halogen content (chlorine + fluorine) is strictly limited per MIL-STD-2132 or equivalent specifications. Sulfur is limited. Material certification with each lot of penetrant.</p><h2>Common Audit Findings</h2><p>(a) Penetrant materials not on QPL. (b) Sensitivity level not appropriate for the application. (c) Process control checks not performed at frequency required. (d) Reference panel verification missing. (e) UV-A intensity below 1000 µW/cm². (f) System verification monthly interval exceeded. (g) Examiner certification not aligned with NAS 410 for aerospace.</p>`,
  revisionsHistory: [
    { year: '2021', notes: 'E1417/E1417M-21; updated process control intervals; aligned with NAS 410 latest revision.' },
    { year: '2016', notes: 'E1417/E1417M-16; added Level 4 ultra-high sensitivity classification.' },
    { year: '2013', notes: 'Major revision; expanded Annex A1 process control.' },
  ],
  relatedStandards: ['astm-e165', 'asme-section-v-article-6', 'iso-3452'],
  applicableCertifications: [
    { label: 'ASNT Certification (PT Levels I, II, III)', href: CERTS.asnt },
  ],
  applicableMethods: [
    { label: 'Liquid Penetrant Testing (PT)', href: METHODS.pt },
    { label: 'ISO 3452 PT Specification', href: METHODS.iso3452 },
  ],
  issuingBody: 'ASTM',
  category: 'practice',
});

standards.push({
  slug: 'astm-e1444',
  code: 'ASTM E1444',
  displayTitle: 'ASTM E1444 — Standard Practice for Magnetic Particle Testing',
  shortDescription: 'Aerospace-grade magnetic particle practice — defines sensitivity classification, qualified products, system verification, and rigorous process control for ferromagnetic aerospace parts.',
  scope: `<p>ASTM E1444 — <strong>Standard Practice for Magnetic Particle Testing</strong> — is the aerospace-grade MT practice, the counterpart to ASTM E1417 (PT). E1444 imposes stricter requirements than ASTM E709 — qualified particle materials, system verification with Ketos rings, daily process control, and rigorous documentation. E1444 is the dominant MT standard for aerospace ferromagnetic components: landing gear, engine components, turbine disks, and structural fasteners.</p><p>The current edition is ASTM E1444/E1444M-22. E1444 references AMS 3040 (qualified particle materials), MIL-STD-1949, and is invoked by major aerospace prime contractors and Nadcap NDT accreditation.</p>`,
  keyRequirements: `<h2>Magnetic Particle Materials — AMS 3040</h2><p>Particles shall be from the qualified products list per AMS 3040. Wet fluorescent particles are the dominant aerospace choice — particle size, suspension medium, and fluorescent dye are all controlled. Specific brand/lot tracking is typical.</p><h2>System Verification — Ketos Ring (Annex A4)</h2><p>The Ketos ring (Annex A4) is the primary system verification artifact for MT — a steel ring with calibrated holes drilled at different distances from the surface. The ring is magnetized with a central conductor passing a specified current (typically 1400 A) and inspected with the system. The number of "indications visible" per Annex A4 establishes system sensitivity. The ring shall pass at the system frequency (start of shift, after any change, monthly minimum).</p><h2>Magnetization Techniques (8)</h2><p>Same techniques as ASTM E709 but with stricter parameter control for aerospace. Specific applications:</p><ul><li><strong>Central conductor on hollow parts:</strong> Used for landing gear, bushings, and tubes.</li><li><strong>Coil method for shafts and long parts:</strong> Coil ampere-turns calculated per Section 8.4.</li><li><strong>Head shot:</strong> Used in stationary MT benches.</li><li><strong>Multi-directional MT (MDMT):</strong> Simultaneous longitudinal + circumferential field — a key aerospace technique that reduces inspection time and ensures full-coverage in one shot.</li></ul><h2>Multi-Directional MT (Annex A5)</h2><p>Multi-directional MT uses overlapping AC and 3-phase rectified DC fields at perpendicular orientations. Field strength is verified independently for each component. Verification panel (typically combined with Ketos ring) demonstrates that all orientations are revealed.</p><h2>Particle Concentration (10)</h2><p>Centrifuge tube settling volume: 0.1 to 0.4 mL settled per 100 mL bath for fluorescent (more typical 0.1-0.4 mL), 0.1-0.4 mL for visible. Bath samples drawn daily; lot tracking maintained. Out-of-range concentration is corrected by addition of concentrate or fresh bath.</p><h2>UV-A Intensity (11.3)</h2><p>Per ASTM E1444 11.3, UV-A intensity ≥ 1000 µW/cm² at the part surface. Lamp warm-up minimum 5 minutes before inspection. UV-A intensity is verified at start of shift and after any UV-A bulb change. Bulb wear gradually reduces intensity; replacement is typical at 50-70% of new intensity.</p><h2>White Light (11.4)</h2><p>Maximum 2 fc ambient white light in fluorescent inspection area. Some facilities use light-tight booths or curtained zones to control ambient light.</p><h2>Examiner Dark Adaptation</h2><p>Minimum 1 minute in darkened area before fluorescent inspection. Sustained focused inspection — operator productivity is balanced against vision accuracy.</p><h2>Examiner Qualification</h2><p>Per NAS 410 (aerospace) or AIA NAS-410 / EN 4179. For Level 4 sensitivity applications, additional demonstration on representative parts may be required. Vision exams annual.</p><h2>Daily Process Control (Annex A1)</h2><p>Daily checks include:</p><ul><li>UV-A intensity verification.</li><li>White light ambient intensity.</li><li>Hall gauge verification (or equivalent field strength check).</li><li>Centrifuge tube particle concentration.</li><li>Reference standard verification (Ketos ring or MDMT panel).</li><li>Bath fluid integrity (water content, contamination).</li><li>Demagnetization verification.</li></ul><p>Records retained 7 years (typical) or per operator's QMS.</p><h2>Surface Preparation (7)</h2><p>For aerospace, surface preparation is rigorous. Etching is required on as-machined surfaces to remove smeared metal that closes cracks. Surface temperature shall be within 40°F to 110°F (procedure may extend range for specific applications).</p><h2>Demagnetization (13)</h2><p>Required for aerospace components going to subsequent welding, brazing, machining, or use in instrumented systems. Residual field shall be reduced to typically ≤ 3 Gauss. Demagnetization quality verified with tangential field gauge.</p><h2>Multi-Stage Inspection — Black Light and White Light</h2><p>Some aerospace specifications require both fluorescent MT and visible MT (or follow-up white light verification). Sequence is typically fluorescent first, then white light. Each inspection station is process-controlled.</p><h2>Common Audit Findings</h2><p>(a) Particles not from AMS 3040 QPL. (b) Ketos ring verification not performed at required frequency. (c) UV-A intensity not measured at part surface. (d) MDMT verification panel not used. (e) Process control records incomplete or out-of-range entries not addressed. (f) Examiner NAS 410 certification expired. (g) Demagnetization residual field not measured.</p>`,
  revisionsHistory: [
    { year: '2022', notes: 'E1444/E1444M-22; updated UV-A specifications; aligned with NAS 410 and Nadcap audit checklist updates.' },
    { year: '2016', notes: 'E1444/E1444M-16; expanded MDMT Annex A5 requirements.' },
    { year: '2012', notes: 'Major revision; restructured process control requirements.' },
  ],
  relatedStandards: ['astm-e709', 'asme-section-v-article-7', 'iso-9934'],
  applicableCertifications: [
    { label: 'ASNT Certification (MT Levels I, II, III)', href: CERTS.asnt },
  ],
  applicableMethods: [
    { label: 'Magnetic Particle Testing (MT)', href: METHODS.mt },
    { label: 'ASTM E1444 Radiographic Interpretation (related)', href: METHODS.astme1444 },
    { label: 'ISO 9934 MT Standards', href: METHODS.iso9934 },
  ],
  issuingBody: 'ASTM',
  category: 'practice',
});

